      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 640‑acre farm that became Fearrington was originally bought for just 100 shillings in 1786 by William Cole.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fearrington_Village%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1840 the farm passed to Elijah Cole, and by 1859 it was inherited by his daughter Adelaide and her husband, Edwin Mebane Fearrington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://fearringtonfha.org/history-of-fearrington-village/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Fearrington family chose a buyer who would preserve the farm’s historic values and the special qualities that made Eureka Farm a key part of Chatham County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://fearringtonfha.org/history-of-fearrington-village/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Routes 15 and 501 form the northwestern edge of Fearrington, leading 8 miles north to Chapel Hill and 8 miles south to Pittsboro.",
